WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, Episode 84.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.980
Today is May 29th, 2026.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.940
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.640
A lost ice giant may explain how Jupiter and Uranus

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.540
acquired their moons.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.560
New research points to a vanished third ice giant

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.260
that once orbited between Jupiter and Uranus.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.560
Its gravitational influence could have scattered material

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.300
that later coalesced into the moons we see today.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.640
The study draws on orbital simulations

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.080
comparing current moon systems with scenarios

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.360
that include an extra giant planet.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.220
Evidence comes from the similar compositions

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.160
and orbital patterns of several irregular satellites

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.460
around both worlds.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.980
The work suggests this lost body

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.400
was ejected early in solar system history.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.380
Follow-up dynamical models will test whether

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.660
the same event also shaped the Kuiper belt.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.380
Now shifting to a modern technique

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.500
that weighs planets still forming today.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.180
Researchers at the University of Warwick

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.440
developed a method to weigh forming planets

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.640
by measuring gaps they carve in surrounding dust discs.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.500
The technique uses high-resolution images from Alma

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.020
to map how dust is cleared at specific orbital distances.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.120
Gap width and depth correlate directly

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.920
with the planet's mass through hydrodynamic models.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.860
The approach works on systems still embedded

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.320
in their natal gas, offering a window

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.760
into the first few million years of planet growth.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.500
It avoids the need for direct imaging

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.760
of the planets themselves.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.560
Next steps involve applying the method

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.960
to a larger sample of discs observed by Alma and JWST.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.440
For measuring planets' information to instruments

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.060
designed to map the distant universe in bulk,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.600
a new facility under construction in Western China

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.360
will obtain spectra for thousands of galaxies each night.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.940
The instrument combines a wide field telescope

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.840
with thousands of optical fibers

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.080
feeding individual spectrographs.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.420
It addresses the current imbalance

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.160
where billions of images exist,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.940
but spectra remains scarce.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.220
The design targets faint objects across large sky areas

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.140
to measure red shifts, compositions, and velocities

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:11.980
in one pass.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.420
Once operational, it will map cosmic structure

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.660
on scales previously limited by spectroscopic coverage.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.360
Data releases are expected to begin

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.100
within two years of first light.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.760
From gathering vast new data sets to protecting the hardware

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.320
that will carry those data on deep space missions,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.320
a new memory technology uses fair electric materials

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.060
that retain data without power and resist radiation damage.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.860
Laboratory tests expose the chips to radiation doses

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.160
equivalent to 100 million X-rays

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.620
with no loss of stored information.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.980
Conventional flash memory fails at far lower levels,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.640
limiting deep space missions.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.980
The chips could store science data on probes

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.980
traveling to Jupiter or beyond without heavy shielding.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.780
The design exploits the spontaneous electric polarization

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.100
in certain crystals that remains stable

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.760
under particle bombardment.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.180
Flight hardware development is now under discussion

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.520
with several agencies,

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.080
from radiation hardened electronics to another technology

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.760
that turns precise timing into mass measurements

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:17.840
across galaxies.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.840
Astronomers at the University of Alabama

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.220
in Huntsville used 54 millisecond pulsars

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.720
as precise clocks to detect gravitational perturbations.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.300
Tiny timing shifts revealed the mass of the large

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.660
Magellanic Cloud and the Sagittarius dwarf galaxy,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.020
including their dark matter halos.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.440
The method measures the galaxy's gravitational pull

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.040
as they orbit the Milky Way.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.580
It provides direct mass estimates

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.560
without relying on visible stars or gas alone.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.480
The same network of pulsars could eventually

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.900
map the Milky Way's dark matter distribution.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.460
Additional observations will refine the measurements

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.100
and extend the technique to more distant satellites.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.080
You know what's fascinating about this?

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.780
If the sun were compressed into a sphere

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.840
the size of Manhattan,

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.960
a single teaspoon of its material would weigh

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.080
about a billion tons.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.560
Millicent pulsars are neutron stars

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.660
spinning hundreds of times per second,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.500
sending out radio beams that sweep past Earth

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.700
like lighthouse flashes.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.800
When a passing galaxy tugs on the Milky Way,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.040
the distance between us and the pulsar

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.620
changes by mere meters,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.620
enough to advance or delay the pulse arrival by microseconds.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.120
A network of 54 such clocks spread across the sky

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.100
can isolate these minute shifts

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.040
from the galaxy's overall motion.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.720
The surprising result is that the mass

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.460
of the large Magellanic Cloud,

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.380
including its dark matter halo,

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.600
can be read directly from the timing residuals

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.740
without ever seeing the dark matter itself.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.240
The remaining mystery is why the measured masses

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.420
sometimes differ from those inferred

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.780
from stellar motions alone.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.980
From weighing galaxies to a deeper look

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.480
at how the same pulsar clocks actually work.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.080
A new Glenn rocket exploded during a ground test

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.660
on the launch pad in Florida.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.620
The blast could affect Blue Origin's timeline

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.760
for delivering the Blue Moon lander

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.800
under NASA's Artemis program.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.920
Without major modifications, Falcon Heavy cannot serve

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.880
as a substitute launcher for the mission.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.540
NASA is now assessing whether the setback delay

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.200
is crude lunar landings.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.320
Engineers are reviewing video of the fireball

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.360
to determine the failure's root cause.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.860
A revised schedule is expected

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.600
after the investigation concludes.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.420
From commercial setbacks to steady robotic progress

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.440
on another world,

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.980
mineral layers mapped by curiosity in gale crater

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:48.360
show a transition from wet to drier conditions over time.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.360
The rover's instruments detected clays

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.820
formed in water-rich environments overlaying

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.320
by sulfate-rich rocks that formed as water became scarce.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:59.740
The sequence provides a timeline

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.080
for when Mars lost its surface water.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.400
Orbital data from Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.700
helped place the rover observations in broader context.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.640
Scientists are now correlating these layers

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.560
with climate models of the planet's early atmosphere.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.520
Future samples returned by perseverance

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.760
will allow laboratory confirmation of the mineral ages.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.440
From Mars' climate history to evidence

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.140
that some stars are still consuming their planets.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.600
A study in monthly notices of the Royal Astronomical Society

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.440
presents chemical evidence that young red dwarfs

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.080
ingest nearby planets.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.160
Spectra of the stars show elevated levels

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.040
of rock forming elements that match the composition

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.520
of terrestrial worlds.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.160
The pattern appears strongest in systems still surrounded

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.280
by debris discs.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.040
The finding support models in which inward migration

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.620
brings planets close enough to be accreted.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.820
Observations from several ground-based telescopes

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.100
contributed to the sample of 20 stars.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:02.180
Additional surveys will determine how common

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.460
this process is among low-mass stars.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.960
Space X will fly the Starlink 10-53 mission

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.760
from Cape Canaveral Pad 40 at 8.57 am Eastern Daylight Time.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:17.160
The Falcon 9 first stage will attempt a landing

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:19.620
on a drone ship after delivering the satellites

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.800
to low Earth orbit.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.500
This marks the 49th Starlink dedicated launch of 2026.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.780
The satellites will join the growing constellation

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.960
providing global broadband coverage.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.960
Whether and range constraints will determine

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.360
whether the launch proceeds on the first attempt.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.900
Live coverage begins one hour before liftoff.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.320
The Batagayka crater in Siberia appears

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.120
in a new Copernicus Sentinel-2 image

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.940
as a steep wall depression formed by thawing permafrost.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.620
The feature, known as a mega-slump,

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.500
continues to grow as melting exposes fresh ice-rich ground.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.740
Radar and optical data track its expansion rate

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:00.960
over multiple seasons.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.380
The crater offers a visible record

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.540
of how warming temperatures affect Arctic landscapes.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.460
ESA plans continued monitoring to quantify

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.040
sediment and carbon release.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.720
Sentinel-1 radar observations of South Africa's maize triangle

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.220
reveal how different crops change through the growing season.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.020
The false color composite distinguishes maize, wheat,

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:27.120
and other fields by their radar backscatter signatures.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.620
Seasonal changes in plant height

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.280
and moisture produce distinct temporal patterns.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.200
The data cover the southern hemisphere summer

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.140
when optical satellites often face cloud cover.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.780
Agricultural agencies use the maps

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.940
to estimate yields and plant harvests.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.740
A bright meteor was recorded by a feverless camera

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:50.280
at 10.33 pm local time on May 25th.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.780
The streak appeared above the volcano

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.340
and lasted several seconds before fading.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:58.220
No fragments have been recovered so far.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.560
The event was unrelated to volcanic activity.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.420
Similar meteors are common,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.580
but rarely captured at this resolution.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.980
Venus and Jupiter dominate the western sky

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.960
after sunset through early June.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.620
The summer triangle rises in the east as twilight fades.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.720
The Milky Way becomes visible once the moon

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.020
sets later in the week.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.300
Observers can follow the planets

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.220
changing separation each evening.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.080
Binoculars reveal the Pleiades near Venus.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.120
The full moon on May 31st qualifies as a blue moon

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.720
because it is the second full moon in a calendar month.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.700
It rises in the constellation Afiyukus.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.140
Observers in the Americas will see it low in the southeast

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:39.880
at sunset.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.480
The moon will appear slightly orange near the horizon

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.180
due to atmospheric scattering.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.460
No special equipment is needed beyond clear skies.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.600
The next blue moon occurs in 2028.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.900
The fourth magnitude star, new draconus

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:56.160
in the head of Draco,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.200
separates into two nearly identical components

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:00.500
with small binoculars.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.220
The paralyze 100 light years away

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:05.080
and shares common proper motion.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.140
Both stars are slightly cooler than the sun.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.800
The double is a classic test for steady atmospheric conditions.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.520
It remains visible through summer evenings.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.740
Keep an eye on follow-up observations

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.500
from the pulsar timing network

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.320
as more data refine the dark matter maps.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.840
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

